{"causes":["The cultural and intellectual context of early Christian Egypt during the 1st millennium CE, characterized by a complex interplay of Jewish, Greek, and Roman influences."],"effects":["Influence on the development of Gnosticism and other apocalyptic movements within early Christianity."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"nag-hammadi-library","sourceName":"The Nag Hammadi Library","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"gospel-of-thomas","targetName":"Gospel of Thomas","context":"This collection of texts, discovered in 1945, provides a unique window into early Christian thought and practice."},{"sourceSlug":"alexandria","sourceName":"Alexandria","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"egypt","targetName":"Egypt","context":"As a key center of early Christian learning and culture, Alexandria played a significant role in the transmission of Jewish and Christian texts."},{"sourceSlug":"jesus-christ","sourceName":"Jesus Christ","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"early-christian-movement","targetName":"Early Christian Movement","context":"The teachings of Jesus, as recorded in the Gospel of Thomas, have shaped the course of early Christianity and continue to inspire adherents today."},{"sourceSlug":"gnosticism","sourceName":"Gnosticism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"early-christian-movement","targetName":"Early Christian Movement","context":"The Gospel of Thomas's unique blend of mysticism and apocalyptic vision has contributed to the development of Gnostic thought within early Christianity."},{"sourceSlug":"early-christian-movement","sourceName":"Early Christian Movement","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"christianity","targetName":"Christianity","context":"The Gospel of Thomas, as a key text within the Nag Hammadi library, provides valuable insights into the diversity of early Christian thought and practice."},{"sourceSlug":"alexandria","sourceName":"Alexandria","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"christianity","targetName":"Christianity","context":"As a center of early Christian learning and culture, Alexandria played a significant role in the transmission of Jewish and Christian texts."},{"sourceSlug":"nag-hammadi-library","sourceName":"The Nag Hammadi Library","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"gospel-of-thomas","targetName":"Gospel of Thomas","context":"This collection of texts, discovered in 1945, provides a unique window into early Christian thought and practice."},{"sourceSlug":"egypt","sourceName":"Egypt","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"christianity","targetName":"Christianity","context":"As a key center of early Christian learning and culture, Egypt played a significant role in the transmission of Jewish and Christian texts."},{"sourceSlug":"judaism","sourceName":"Judaism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"early-christian-movement","targetName":"Early Christian Movement","context":"The teachings of Jesus, as recorded in the Gospel of Thomas, have been shaped by Jewish scripture and tradition."}],"places":[{"name":"Egypt","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Alexandria","role":"Center of learning and culture"},{"name":"Nag Hammadi","role":"Discovery site"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Gospel of Thomas","type":"Reference article","slug":"gospel-of-thomas"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q131546","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Thomas"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Gospel of Thomas contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Gospel of Thomas is a non-canonical collection of 114 sayings attributed to Jesus, discovered as part of the Nag Hammadi library in December 1945 near Nag Hammadi, Upper Egypt, and first published in full in 1959 — one of the most significant finds in the history of early Christian scholarship, and the most complete example of the genre of 'sayings gospel' in contrast to the narrative Gospels of the New Testament. The text exists in a Coptic translation (from the Greek original) and is dated by scholars to the 1st or 2nd century CE — with debate about whether it preserves authentic early Jesus sayings (possibly predating the Synoptic Gospels) or represents a 2nd-century Gnostic reworking. The text begins: 'These are the secret sayings which the living Jesus spoke, and which Didymos Judas Thomas wrote down' — the attribution to 'Doubting Thomas' (as the twin of Jesus in some Syriac traditions) gives the text its name.

The Gospel of Thomas contains a number of sayings that parallel the canonical Gospels (the parables of the Mustard Seed, the Pearl, the Sower) and others that have no canonical parallel — including the famous 'split a piece of wood, and I am there; lift up the stone, and you will find me there' (Saying 77) and 'If you bring forth what is within you, what you bring forth will save you; if you do not bring forth what is within you, what you do not bring forth will destroy you' (Saying 70), which have a distinctly Gnostic, inward-turning spirituality. The Gospel of Thomas has no narrative, no Passion, no resurrection — it is a purely wisdom text, presenting Jesus as a revealer of hidden knowledge (gnosis) rather than a saviour through sacrifice.

The Gospel of Thomas is one of the most debated texts in New Testament scholarship — the question of its date and its relationship to the Synoptic Gospels is central to the reconstruction of the history of early Christianity. If it preserves an early stratum of Jesus sayings independent of the Synoptics, it provides invaluable evidence for the diversity of early Christianity; if it is a 2nd-century Gnostic composition, it illustrates the development of Gnostic Christianity from the canonical tradition.","subjects":["Gnostic Christianity","Classical Era","New Testament Apocrypha","Early Christianity","Jesus","Christian Texts","Nag Hammadi","Biblical Scholarship"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Gospel of Thomas offers a unique window into the diversity of early Christian thought and practice, providing valuable insights into the cultural and intellectual context of early Christian Egypt.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
